      "category": "brakes",
      "title": "Four-Wheel Drum Brakes — No Stopping Power for the Horsepower",
      "description": "Early Chevelles left the factory with four-wheel drums (front discs were not even optional until 1967, and rare after). The single-circuit master cylinder on pre-1967 cars means one leak equals no brakes, and the drums fade badly under repeated or hard stops — a real safety weak point on a car making 300-450 lb-ft. As restorers note, even a basic disc package out-stops the best drum brake ever built. Restorers upgrading the engine and not the brakes end up with a fast car that can't stop.",
      "solution": "Convert the front to discs and the master to a dual-circuit (tandem) cylinder. The proven budget path is the 'short-spindle' swap: machine/adapt the stock 64-66 spindles (or bolt on 69-72 drum-brake spindles) to accept 69-72 single-piston calipers with 11-inch rotors. For a bigger upgrade, run tall B-body/F-body spindles for better geometry, add a correct proportioning valve, and step to a power booster. Complete bolt-on kits (SS396, The Right Stuff, CPP) exist for 64-72 that retain 14-15 inch wheels — pick the kit to match the wheel size and keep a residual/proportioning valve for the drum rear.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Long pedal travel and weak, fading stops",
        "Pulling to one side under braking",
        "Total brake loss from a single leak (single-circuit cars)",
        "Brakes overwhelmed after an engine/power upgrade",
        "Hard pedal / no power assist on manual-drum cars"
      ],
